  "body": "Found in the high-tier review of PR #815 (#813): https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/815#pullrequestreview-5856868422\n\n**The gap:**\n- A work-turning rotary about **Y** (a table B: vector `[0,1,0]`, or none and named B) is treated as off X. It gets **no swing check, no refusal and no load note**, so a `G0 B..` index could swing the work into the retracted tool unjudged.\n- It's the same risk as the A case (#804/#810, #813/#815): a vertical tool tip at height h is at least h from any horizontal line through the WCS origin.\n- A rotary named A whose *stated* vector is off X falls through the same way.\n- **C is safe:** turning about a vertical line keeps every point's height.\n\n**Today it's latent:** no shipped machine has a work-turning B (millturn's C carries the head), and nothing judged any swing before #804.\n\n**Expected:**\n- Generalise `rotary_line` and the swept radius in `crates/app/src/posting_index_swing.rs` to any work-turning rotary about a HORIZONTAL line through the WCS origin.\n- Refuse by name when `nose_to_axis` is unstated, exactly as for A.\n- Extend the load note (`project/swing_note.rs`) to those rotaries too.\n\nEffort: high (a post-gate safety law).\n\n**Done means:** a table-B machine (a test `.mch`) gets the same index-swing judgement as the microARC A, with refusal and clear pins, and a horizontal-line A with an off-X vector is judged. Pinned, and a revert check shows each pin fails.\n\nPart of #785.\n",
